ponent**, not a rehabilitation candidate. The subject is an 850 sq ft single-family structure built in 1940 — 86 years old with DCAD-recorded depreciation at 45%. At 850 sq ft on the East 50 Feet of Lot 49, Block 5823 of the Urbandale Addition No. 1, the footprint is tight but the underlying land value in the 75227 zip (Pleasant Grove submarket) is the real asset here. With a market value of $193,300 on what is likely a modest land-to-improvement ratio given the age and depreciation, the improvement component is functionally subordinate — this structure has major systems (roof, HVAC, electrical, plumbing) that are at or well past end-of-life for a 1940-era wood-frame build with no documented renovation history in DCAD. No homestead exemption is on record, which is a critical signal: **Juan F. and Sanjuana Trevino are not living at 7111 Toland St.** Both defendants list 9640 Partridge Loop, Apt. 17892, Laredo, TX 78045 as their address of record — approximately 430 miles southwest of the subject property. This is an **absentee-owner situation**, likely a legacy hold from the December 17, 1986 deed transfer, which means this asset has been in the Trevino name for roughly **40 years** with no apparent active management or equity extraction strategy. Payment history reveals layered prior involvement: **Midland Mortgage Co** appears twice with payments in 1999, suggesting the property carried a mortgage in the late 1990s that was presumably paid off or discharged. More notably, **LV Assets Company LLC** made a payment against the 2022 tax year, which is the oldest delinquency year in this suit — this is a lien investor or tax servicer that may have partially resolved or advanced against that year, but delinquency continued for 2023, 2024, and 2025. That pattern suggests either a failed redemption attempt by a third party or a one-year cure that collapsed. The owners appear disengaged, geographically distant, and holding a depreciating asset with zero','\n',char(10)),NULL